Checkpoint and fold before the pusher goes.

What the next start of a project replays from is the newest checkpoint
the store holds a fold for, not the newest byte it holds. A node that is
stopped in the middle of a checkpoint cycle therefore hands its
successor every byte written since the last one, and the successor walks
them a store read at a time. On server3 that was an hour of WAL and a
project that could not be attached again at all: seven attempts in a row
each started at the same redo point, replayed 15 MB in the first ten
seconds and then about 20 KB per ten seconds after that, and each was
killed by the sixty second attach deadline (zou #457).

So the pusher, once it is drained and has been asked to stop, asks for a
checkpoint and stays up until it has folded it. The request cannot be a
waiting one: flushing a buffer waits on the durable LSN the pusher
publishes, so a pusher asleep inside RequestCheckpoint would be the
other half of its own deadlock. It asks and carries on with its loop,
which is what the checkpointer needs from it, and leaves when a fold
newer than the checkpoint that was current at the request has landed.

ZOU_SHUTDOWN_SETTLE_SECS bounds the wait, ten seconds by default, and 0
turns it off. Going anyway is always safe: nothing acked is at risk
either way, the cost of giving up is the replay this is here to avoid.

Measured on a laptop against a local directory store, 20000 rows
inserted and the node stopped with SIGINT. The settle spans 324 ms from
the request to the fold landing, 211 ms of checkpoint and 113 ms of
fold, and the whole stop goes from 280 ms to 346 ms because the rest of
the shutdown runs alongside it. The store's redo point ends 208 bytes
from the end of its stream instead of 6.5 MB back, and the next start
replays 4 records against 6.5 MB, redo done in 0.00 s against 0.71 s, on
a store whose reads are a memcpy.

